<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Khatyn massacre</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1943">1943</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Khatyn massacre was the destruction of the village of Khatyn in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belarus">Belarus</a> by a German auxiliary police battalion and SS troops on 22 March 1943. Its 149 inhabitants, including 75 children, were burned in a barn or shot in reprisal for a partisan attack. A memorial complex opened on the site in 1969 commemorates them and the hundreds of other Belarusian villages destroyed during the occupation.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Khatyn, a village of 26 houses in Lahoysk district of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minsk">Minsk</a> region, about 50 km from Minsk, suffered a fate common in occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belarus">Belarus</a>. At least 5,295 Belarusian settlements were burned by the Nazis, often with all their inhabitants, as punishment for helping partisans; in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vitebsk">Vitebsk</a> region 243 villages were burned twice. Over 2,000,000 people, almost a quarter of the population, were killed in Belarus during the three years of occupation.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>On 22 March 1943 men of Schutzmannschaft Battalion 118, made up mostly of Ukrainian and other Soviet collaborators, were repairing a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telephone">telephone</a> line near Kozyri, 6 km from Khatyn, when Soviet partisans attacked them, killing four policemen, among them the company commander Hauptmann Hans Woellke. The partisans fled towards Khatyn. Platoon commander Vasyl Meleshko called for help from the SS-Sonderbataillon Dirlewanger, to which the battalion was subordinated. Meanwhile 26 of a group of woodcutters detained at Kozyri were killed near the village of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quba">Guba</a> when they panicked and tried to flee.</p><p>Dirlewanger's men under SS-Sturmbannführer Praefke and the battalion under Hryhoriy Vasiura surrounded Khatyn and shelled the partisans sheltering there; by the afternoon 34 partisans had been killed. The surviving villagers were driven into a shed, which was covered with straw and set on fire, and those who broke out through the doors were cut down by machine guns. The village was looted and burned. Eight inhabitants survived, among them the smith Yuzif Kaminsky and several children.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Bruno Pavel, the officer who ordered the massacre, was tried at the Riga Trial in 1946 and executed. Meleshko was executed in 1975, and Vasiura, tried in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minsk">Minsk</a> in 1986, in 1987; his trial received little publicity, as Soviet leaders worried about unity between the Belarusian and Ukrainian peoples. In 1969 Khatyn became the national war memorial of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byelorussian-ssr">Byelorussian SSR</a>, with a statue of Kaminsky carrying his dying son and a Cemetery of villages of 185 tombs. The historian Norman Davies argues that the Soviet authorities used it to create confusion with the Katyn massacre.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Khatyn massacre” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>German occupation from 1941 to 1944 killed perhaps a quarter of the population and destroyed hundreds of villages, among them <b>Khatyn</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/byelorussian-ssr">Byelorussian SSR</a></li></ul></section>
